course: dictionary
lesson: 2936
title: Dreadfully: very, or very badly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dreadfully sounds scary, but it usually does not mean full of dread. Often it just means very. «I'm dreadfully sorry.» means I am very sorry. Try swapping in very: «I'm very sorry» still makes sense, so very is the meaning here.
explain: The second meaning is very badly. «The team played dreadfully.» The word played is the clue: the team did not play well, they played in a very bad way. So dreadfully means badly here.
explain: Old books such as Alice in Wonderland use dreadfully for very, as in «a dreadfully ugly child». It sounds polite and a little old-fashioned today. Always check the words around it to see which meaning fits.
fact: Dreadfully is an adverb: it tells you how much or how badly something happens.
fact: Meaning 1: very or extremely, as in «I'm dreadfully sorry.»
fact: Meaning 2: very badly, as in «The team played dreadfully.»
fact: In Alice, the sentence «it would have made a dreadfully ugly child» means very ugly, not scary.
fact: A good check is to swap in very or badly and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The rain was dreadfully heavy, and we stayed inside all day.» What does dreadfully mean here? | Slowly | Badly | Happily | *Very
exercise: «The footballer played dreadfully and the team lost.» What does dreadfully suggest? | *Very badly | Very happily | Very quietly | Very well
exercise: «I'm dreadfully sorry I broke your pencil.» Which word could replace dreadfully without changing the meaning? | Happily | *Very | Quickly | Never
test: «Alice said the pig would have made a dreadfully ugly child.» What does dreadfully mean here? | *Very | Quietly | Badly at drawing | Happily
test: Which sentence uses dreadfully to mean very badly? | *«She sang dreadfully out of tune.» | «The dog was dreadfully happy to see us.» | «I'm dreadfully sorry for the mess.»
exam: In «I'm dreadfully sorry», dreadfully means which word? | Frightened | *Very | Badly | Quietly
